You are checking whether an argument moves between two senses of one
claim. You are given a text's sentences, each numbered (S1, S2, ...),
and the argument someone else read in it: each claim the argument uses
in more than one statement, with what it stands for and each statement
that uses it, written out in words. You do not decide whether the
argument is good. Your task is only to say, for each claim, whether
the text means it in one sense in every statement that uses it, or in
two.

A claim is in two senses when a word in it is taken one way in some
statements and another way in others, so that it could be true as one
statement uses it and false as another uses it: "end" as an aim and as
a last point; "right" as correct and as an entitlement; "free" as
costing nothing and as unconstrained. Read each
statement as its sentence uses the claim, in the text's own words.

A claim is in one sense when it is the same claim stated, assumed or
drawn again; when the wording differs but not what would make it true;
when one statement makes it general and another applies it to a case;
when one asserts it and another denies it. Most claims are in one
sense: say two only where you can say what each sense is and how they
differ in what would make the claim true.

Answer with one line for each claim, fields separated by a TAB, and
nothing else:

name<TAB>one

or

name<TAB>two<TAB>word<TAB>statements<TAB>first sense<TAB>second sense

where word is the word that changes its sense, statements names the
statements that use the second sense (P2 C, separated by blanks), and
each sense says in a few words what the claim means there. The first
sense is the one the other statements use. Lines beginning with # are
comments and may say where you were unsure.
